<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Fire losses are bigger than they look</h2>
<p>Flame damage is obvious. Smoke and soot are not. They travel through ductwork, wall cavities, attics and closets, settling on surfaces far from the fire. Heat can warp windows, fixtures and framing in rooms that look untouched. A carrier estimate that focuses on the burned room often leaves the rest of the house underscoped.</p>
<h2>Where fire estimates are commonly disputed</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cleaning versus replacement</strong> of cabinets, flooring, trim and contents with lingering odor.</li>
<li><strong>Smoke sealing and repainting</strong> limited to a few rooms when soot traveled through the entire HVAC system.</li>
<li><strong>Ductwork and HVAC</strong> cleaning or replacement after heavy soot.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades</strong> triggered by a major rebuild, which may fall under ordinance or law coverage.</li>
<li><strong>Contents</strong> undervalued through heavy depreciation or missing items.</li>
<li><strong>Additional living expense</strong> that runs out before the rebuild is done.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How we approach fire and smoke appraisal</h2>
<p>We document smoke patterns room by room, review any cleaning tests and indoor air findings, and scope the rebuild in full. For contents, we help organize an inventory with realistic ages and replacement prices, since depreciation on contents is where many fire settlements lose ground.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Wildfire is its own category</b>Fires that start outside, especially in the Black Hills, raise different questions about ash, smoke and landscaping. See our <a href="/appraiser-services/black-hills-wildfire-claim-appraiser/">Black Hills wildfire claim appraisal</a> page.</div>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long do I have to inventory my contents?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Policies set deadlines for submitting proof of loss and inventories, and carriers may extend them on request. Ask in writing and keep the response.</p></div></details><details><summary>The carrier wants to clean items I think should be replaced. Can appraisal decide that?</summary><div class="ans"><p>Whether an item can be restored to its pre-loss condition, and at what cost, is often a valuation question a panel can address.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you work with fire restoration contractors?</summary><div class="ans"><p>We review their scope and pricing, but we stay independent. Our valuation is our own.</p></div></details></div>
